    "product_id": "928151ae-8eac-4b79-af55-dc9b7aa0e2ae",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 261107\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n407 AM MST Wed Aug 26 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect through Saturday across \n  much of the area.\n\n- Lower desert highs in excess of 110 degrees and overnight lows \n  in the eighties to lower nineties will be common this week, \n  resulting in Major to Extreme Heat Risk.\n\n- Monsoon storm chances will be very limited before a pattern \n  change occurs this weekend, which will result in an increase in \n  moisture and promote temperatures dropping closer to late August\n  normals by Sunday or early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nThe subtropical ridge can been seen positioned along the AZ/NM \nboarder advecting drier air into the lower deserts. The drier \nconditions will to last through the end of the week, with \nforecasted PWATs expected to fall near 1\" in south central AZ and \neven below 1\" in the eastern high terrain by Wednesday. This is \naccompanied by forecasted mixing ratios also falling to around 6-8\ng/kg. However, some isolated, terrain-anchored thunderstorms may \ndevelop each afternoon for the next few days before a pattern \nchange occurs heading into this weekend, drawing in better \nmoisture from over Sonora/the Gulf of California.\n\nThe more note worthy condition for this week will be the heat. H5 \nheights will peak Wednesday-Thursday with heights ranging between 595-\n598 dam, or above the 99th percentile of climatology. Many areas \nacross the lower deserts will be flirting with record high \ntemperatures, with Phoenix showing around a 50% chance of at least \ntying the record, and a 20% chance of surpassing the record high of \n117F set in 2011 on Wednesday. Then on Thursday Phoenix has about \nanother 50% chance of tying and a 30% chance of surpassing the \nrecord of 114F set in 2023. Needless to say this week is going to be \nhot with the lower deserts region ranging between 112F-118F, and \nvery little relief with the overnight lows remaining in the upper \n80s to lower 90s. Widespread Major to Extreme HeatRisk will continue \nthrough the end of the week, as a result the Extreme Heat Warning \nwill also remain in effect through the end of the week and even into \nthe weekend. Ensure to stay hydrated and follow heat safety tips. \n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nThe subtropical ridge is still expected to weaken and shift back \neastwards by Friday according to ensembles. This is due to a \ntroughing pattern pushing into the NW CONUS and along the West \nCoast. As the trough pushes eastward, PWATs will begin to improve, \nreaching near 1.5\"-1.7\" by Saturday and lasting through at least the \nweekend. Mixing ratios will also significantly improve going from \nmid week's 6-8 g/kg to 10-12 g/kg by this weekend, generating better \nsupport for storm formation in the lower deserts. With that being \nsaid NBM/WPC continue to show PoPs over the weekend between 20-35% \nacross South-Central AZ, and now even up to 50% in the eastern high \nterrain. WIth these PoPs the NBM/WPC QPE totals over the weekend are \nforecasted between 0.10\"-0.25\" to locally higher in the higher \nterrain areas. Continued monitoring of this system will be needed \nbut models continue to show support for activity developing this \nweekend. In other good news, temperatures are forecast to drop \ncloser to late August normals by Sunday and into early next week as \nfalling midlevel heights and an increase in moisture/cloud cover \nover the eastern CWA support a cooling trend (more pronounced across \nSouth-Central AZ).\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1105Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nNo major aviation weather concerns under FEW-SCT mid/high cloud \ndecks can be expected throughout the TAF period. The overall wind\npattern will continue to exhibit the typical diurnal tendencies\nwith the easterly wind shift at KPHX likely around 12z. Heading\ninto early this evening, wind directions become highly uncertain \nas there are indications from guidance of potentially a couple of \noutflows from storm activity north and east of Phoenix and across \nsoutheast AZ impacting the terminals with one outflow from the \nnortheast and another from the southeast. Confidence is low of \nthese outflows materializing and surviving into the Phoenix area \nand thus are not included in the current TAF.\n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation weather concerns under FEW-SCT mid/high cloud\ndecks can be expected throughout the TAF period. Winds will mostly\noscillate between the southeast and west with overall speeds\nranging between 7-12 kts.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHot and seasonally dry conditions will prevail for much of the\nrest of the week. Strong high pressure will settle over the \nregion starting today with dry conditions continuing throughout \nthe workweek. Afternoon minimum humidities will mostly fall into \nthe teens through the rest of the week before improving back above\n20% this weekend, except the western CA districts. Temperatures \nwill be around 5-10 degrees above normal, with lower desert highs \neclipsing 110 degrees nearly every afternoon through at least \nFriday. Outside of distant thunderstorm outflows, expect overall \nlight winds (below 15 mph) following typical diurnal tendencies \nwith some modest afternoon upslope gustiness.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\nRecord highs:\n\nDate       Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro\n----       -------         ----         --------- \nAug 26   117 in 2011    115 in 2020    115 in 1981\nAug 27   114 in 2023    120 in 1981    117 in 1981\nAug 28   117 in 2023    118 in 2023    117 in 2009\nAug 29   116 in 2023    117 in 1954    117 in 2005\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Saturday for AZZ530>555-559-\n     560.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ556-561-562.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Saturday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n     Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ561-563-564-\n     568.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Whittock/Ryan \nAVIATION...Lojero/Kuhlman \nFIRE WEATHER...Whittock/Ryan \nCLIMATE...18/Kuhlman\n",
